    'Lord of the Flies by William Golding (first published 1954) remains in copyright. The UK rights are held by Faber & Faber Ltd; the William Golding Estate is represented by United Agents. This page is published in the United Kingdom and relies on the fair dealing exception for criticism, review and quotation under sections 30(1) and 30(1ZA) of the Copyright, Designs and Patents Act 1988.',
  'rev.texts.lotf.extract.copyright_p2':
    "Each verbatim fragment is fifteen words or fewer, taken from the novel's widely available text, used solely as the object of analytical commentary, and clearly attributed to Golding (Chapter 9). No continuous extract is reproduced; the scene is otherwise summarised in our own paraphrase. The amount used is no more than is necessary for the educational purpose of teaching close-reading technique to GCSE and IGCSE students.",
