  'rev.texts.aic.byline': 'by J.B. Priestley - 1945',
  'rev.texts.aic.back': 'Back to An Inspector Calls',
  'rev.texts.aic.fair_dealing':
    'Short quotations (≤15 words each) reproduced under the fair dealing provision of the Copyright, Designs and Patents Act 1988 for the purpose of criticism, review and educational study. An Inspector Calls © J.B. Priestley Estate. Full text available from your school or local library.',
  'rev.texts.aic.acts.title': 'An Inspector Calls - Acts Analysis',
  'rev.texts.aic.acts.intro':
    'Detailed analysis of all three acts with key quotations, language techniques and dramatic devices. Every quote is 15 words or fewer.',
  'rev.texts.aic.acts.language_techniques_act': 'Language Techniques - Act {n}',
  'rev.texts.aic.acts.dramatic_techniques_act': 'Dramatic Techniques - Act {n}',
  'rev.texts.aic.acts.key_quotes_act': 'Key Quotes - Act {n}',
  'rev.texts.aic.acts.continue_sub': 'Explore essay plans and key quotes for An Inspector Calls.',
  'rev.texts.aic.acts_link': 'Acts Analysis',
  'rev.texts.aic.characters.title': 'An Inspector Calls - Characters',
  'rev.texts.aic.characters.intro':
    'Complete character analysis for all seven key characters. Each section includes a detailed overview, character arc, key quotes with AO2 analysis, theme links, and exam technique advice.',
  'rev.texts.aic.characters.exam_technique_body':
    'In GCSE literature, character questions always require you to discuss Priestley’s intentions (AO1/AO3) and analyse language and structure (AO2). For every character point, ask: what is Priestley trying to show the audience through this character?',
  'rev.texts.aic.characters.continue_sub':
    'Explore themes, key quotes, and context for An Inspector Calls.',
  'rev.texts.aic.context.title': 'An Inspector Calls - Context',
  'rev.texts.aic.context.intro':
    'Historical and social context for GCSE literature. Covers the 1912 setting, 1945 writing date, Priestley’s socialism, the welfare state, the class system, women’s rights, and the Labour landslide.',
  'rev.texts.aic.context.ao3_badge': 'AO3 - Context',
  'rev.texts.aic.context.ao3_want_h': 'AO3 - What examiners want',
  'rev.texts.aic.context.ao3_want_body':
    'AO3 rewards you for showing how context shapes meaning. Do not simply list historical facts - explain how the 1912 setting and 1945 writing date affect the audience’s response to the play. The best AO3 answers explain why Priestley made specific choices, not just when things happened.',
  'rev.texts.aic.context.quick_ref_h': 'Quick Reference: 1912 vs 1945',
  'rev.texts.aic.context.continue_sub':
    'Explore characters, themes, and key quotes for An Inspector Calls.',
  'rev.texts.aic.quotes.title': 'An Inspector Calls - Key Quotes',
  'rev.texts.aic.quotes.intro':
    '30 essential quotations with full AO2 language analysis, AO3 context links, and exam technique tips. Filter by act or theme to find the quotes you need.',
  'rev.texts.aic.quotes.exam_technique_body':
    'You do not need to memorise every quote. Choose 3–4 versatile quotes per character that you can use across multiple themes and question types. For each, learn the quote, the speaker, the act, and one strong AO2 analytical point.',
  'rev.texts.aic.essays.title': 'An Inspector Calls - Essay Plans',
  'rev.texts.aic.essays.intro':
    'Five structured GCSE essay plans with thesis statements, paragraph plans, key quotes and analytical points. Use these as a framework for timed practice.',
  'rev.texts.aic.essays.exam_tip_body':
    'These plans follow a four-paragraph structure (plus introduction and conclusion) suitable for a 45-minute GCSE literature response. Each paragraph uses a Point → Quote → Analysis → Link structure. Adapt the plans to match the specific wording of your exam question.',
  'rev.texts.aic.essays.continue_sub': 'Deepen your knowledge with act analysis and key quotes.',
  'rev.texts.aic.extract.badge': 'Extract walkthrough',
  'rev.texts.aic.extract.title': 'The Inspector’s Final Speech - A Close Reading',
  'rev.texts.aic.extract.byline': 'An Inspector Calls, Act 3 - J.B. Priestley',
